        "content": "<h2>The CEO Job Description Nobody Writes</h2><p>If you asked a hundred CEOs what they actually do all day, you'd get a hundred different answers — and most of them would be wrong, or at least incomplete. The formal job description is straightforward enough: set strategy, build the team, allocate capital, represent the company to the board and investors. But the lived reality of the CEO role is far more complicated, more ambiguous, and more psychologically demanding than any description captures. Understanding what the job actually requires — not what the press releases say — is the prerequisite for doing it well.</p><p>The CEO is, in essence, the only person in the organization with no single functional home. Every other executive owns a domain: the CFO owns finance, the CMO owns marketing, the CTO owns technology. The CEO owns the integration of all of them — and that means the CEO's primary work is not functional, it is organizational. The CEO's job is to ensure that the company as a system is working: that the right people are in the right roles, that those people have the information and resources to make good decisions, that the strategy is clear enough to guide daily choices, and that the culture reinforces the behaviors the strategy requires. When any of these elements are missing, the CEO's job is to fix the gap. That's the whole job, in one sentence.</p><h2>The Three Irreducible CEO Functions</h2><p>Across every company stage, every industry, and every business model, three functions are irreducibly the CEO's: deciding who is on the leadership team, deciding what the company will focus on, and deciding how resources are allocated. Everything else can be delegated. These three cannot be. If you outsource hiring your executive team to an executive recruiter without exercising deep judgment on fit, you've abdicated a CEO function. If you let your strategy emerge from departmental priorities rather than making an explicit choice about where you will compete and where you will not, you've abdicated a CEO function. If you let the budget process run bottom-up without making top-down allocation choices that reflect strategic priorities, you've abdicated a CEO function. Most CEO failures, when traced to their root cause, are failures in one of these three irreducible areas.</p><p>Decisions about the leadership team are the highest-leverage CEO activity by a wide margin. A single executive hire or fire decision changes the trajectory of everything that person's function touches — often for years. A great CRO hired at the right moment can accelerate revenue by 40% in 18 months. A mediocre CFO allowed to stay too long during a capital raise can cost the company 200 basis points in valuation. The CEO who spends disproportionate time on hiring and performance management of their direct reports — and who holds a high bar relentlessly — compounds leadership quality over time in ways that no other single activity can match.</p><h2>What CEOs Spend Time On vs. What They Should</h2><p>Research on CEO time allocation consistently reveals a gap between where CEOs spend time and where the highest-value activities lie. CEOs report spending approximately 36% of their time in meetings with direct reports and functional reviews, 25% in external activities (investors, customers, partners, press), 21% in email and administrative communication, and 18% in strategic thinking and planning. The irony is that strategic thinking — the activity that most uniquely requires the CEO and cannot be delegated — is the one most consistently crowded out by meetings and administration. The CEO who has not built a system to protect thinking time will find it consumed entirely by reactive demands.</p><p>The highest-leverage CEO activities are: making major people decisions (hiring, performance management, departures), setting and communicating strategic direction, managing key external relationships (board, investors, major customers, strategic partners), and allocating capital against strategic priorities. These activities compound — the quality of decisions in these areas echoes through the organization for years. In contrast, activities like reviewing operational metrics in detail, attending product demos, or participating in marketing content reviews are lower-leverage and belong to the functional leaders. The CEO who is deep in these activities is either not trusting their team or has not built a team worth trusting — and either diagnosis points back to one of the three irreducible CEO functions.</p><h2>The CEO as Chief Context Setter</h2><p>Perhaps the most underrated CEO function is context setting: ensuring that every person in the organization has enough understanding of the strategy, the competitive situation, the financial position, and the company's values to make good decisions in the CEO's absence. In a 20-person company, the CEO can personally ensure this context exists through daily interaction. In a 200-person company, context setting requires a system — all-hands meetings, written strategic updates, manager communication cascades, cultural rituals that reinforce the company's values and priorities. The CEO who assumes that context exists without actively creating and refreshing it will discover, usually in a crisis, that decisions are being made throughout the organization that are inconsistent with the strategy because the strategy was never clear enough to guide them.</p>",
