        "title": "Unit Economics — The Math That Determines If You Scale or Die",
        "module": "Financial Leadership & Capital Allocation",
        "estimatedMinutes": 30,
        "content": "<h2>Unit Economics Are the Foundation of Scale Decisions</h2><p>Unit economics — the profitability of a single customer or transaction, isolated from the company's overhead — are the fundamental test of whether a business model is viable at scale. A company can grow revenue rapidly while its unit economics are negative: acquiring customers at a cost that exceeds their lifetime value, or delivering service at a cost that exceeds the price. In both cases, more growth makes the problem worse, not better. The CEO who scales before unit economics are positive is not growing a business — they are growing a liability that will eventually demand resolution, usually in the form of a capital raise that dilutes existing shareholders or a restructuring that disrupts the organization.</p><p>The two most important unit economic ratios for B2B subscription businesses are Customer Acquisition Cost (CAC) and Customer Lifetime Value (LTV), and the relationship between them. CAC measures the total cost to acquire one customer — all sales and marketing expense, including compensation, tools, events, and overhead, divided by the number of new customers acquired in the period. LTV measures the total revenue a customer generates over their lifetime with the company, adjusted for gross margin and customer retention rate. The ratio of LTV to CAC — ideally 3:1 or higher for a healthy business — tells the CEO whether the unit economics justify aggressive growth investment or require improvement before scaling.</p><h2>The CAC Payback Period: The CEO's Operational Metric</h2><p>LTV/CAC ratio is the strategic unit economic metric, but CAC payback period is the operational one: how many months of gross margin does it take to recover the cost of acquiring one customer? A CAC payback period of 12 months or less is generally healthy for venture-backed growth companies; 18-24 months is acceptable but requires careful cash management; 24+ months creates significant cash flow risk that limits growth options. The CEO who knows their CAC payback period can make growth investment decisions with precision: if adding $1M to the sales and marketing budget will acquire customers with a 14-month payback at 80% gross margin, the investment is sound. If the payback is 30 months, the same investment is a significant cash risk.</p><p>The most common unit economic mistake for growth-stage CEOs is measuring CAC incorrectly — typically by excluding sales compensation (treating it as overhead rather than acquisition cost), excluding marketing technology costs, or using a 'new logo CAC' that doesn't account for the sales time spent on expansion revenue in existing accounts. Accurate CAC measurement requires including all the expenses that wouldn't exist if the company were not acquiring new customers — including a fully-loaded portion of sales leadership, marketing leadership, and the technology that supports acquisition. Companies that measure CAC too narrowly tend to underestimate the true cost of growth and over-invest in acquisition before their unit economics can support it.</p><h2>Net Revenue Retention: The Unit Economic That Compounds</h2><p>For subscription businesses, the single most powerful unit economic metric is Net Revenue Retention (NRR): the percentage of revenue retained from the existing customer base after accounting for expansions, contractions, and churned customers. An NRR above 100% means the existing customer base grows without any new customer acquisition — the expansion from existing customers exceeds the revenue lost to churn. This creates the compounding dynamic that distinguishes the most valuable subscription businesses: the customer base of Year 1 is worth more in Year 2, even with no new sales activity.</p><p>NRR below 90% is a significant warning signal: the company is losing more than 10% of its customer revenue annually, which means it must run fast on new customer acquisition just to maintain flat revenue. At 80% NRR, the company loses 20% of its existing base annually — a treadmill that makes sustainable growth very difficult to achieve and very expensive to maintain. The CEO who diagnoses low NRR and addresses its root causes (product gaps, customer success underinvestment, ICP misalignment) before scaling growth investment is making one of the highest-ROI strategic decisions available at the growth stage.</p>",
