        "title": "Capital Allocation — Investing Across a Growing Business",
        "module": "Financial Leadership & Capital Allocation",
        "estimatedMinutes": 30,
        "content": "<h2>Capital Allocation Is the CEO's Most Consequential Financial Decision</h2><p>Of all the financial decisions a CEO makes, capital allocation — where the company invests its resources — has the most lasting consequences. Hiring decisions, product investments, market expansions, and operational infrastructure commitments all require capital, and the choices among them determine which capabilities the company builds and which it foregoes. The CEO who allocates capital well builds a company that compounds its strengths over time. The CEO who allocates capital poorly builds an organization where resources are spread across too many priorities, no single capability reaches world-class, and the organization as a whole is weaker than the sum of its parts.</p><p>Capital allocation at the growth stage is complicated by the fact that almost all the opportunities appear more compelling than the capital available to fund them. The company could invest in a new product line, or in accelerating marketing in an existing market, or in building an enterprise sales team, or in international expansion, or in reducing customer churn through customer success investment. All of these might be valuable. The CEO's job is not to identify which of these investments are good — it's to identify which ones are right for the specific moment in the company's development, given the capital available and the strategic priorities. This requires an explicit capital allocation framework, not just an intuitive sense of what the company should do next.</p><h2>The Three Allocation Categories</h2><p>A useful framework for growth-stage capital allocation divides investments into three categories. Core maintenance: the investment required to maintain the quality and performance of the existing business. These are not growth investments — they are the baseline required to sustain current revenue and customer satisfaction. If this category is underfunded, the existing business deteriorates, and growth investments are building on an eroding foundation. Core growth: investment in accelerating the existing business model — more sales capacity, more marketing in proven channels, more customer success resources for the existing product. These investments have measurable returns from existing data and relatively predictable outcomes. Strategic bets: investment in new products, new markets, new business models, or new capabilities that are not yet proven at scale. These have higher expected returns if successful but also higher risk and longer payback periods.</p><p>The allocation balance among these three categories should reflect the company's stage and strategic confidence. Early-stage companies with unproven models should allocate more to strategic bets (figuring out what works) and less to scaling a model that isn't yet proven. Companies with proven models should allocate more to core growth (scaling what works) while maintaining strategic bet investment to build the next growth platform. The failure mode most common in growth-stage companies is allocating too much to strategic bets before the core model is proven — creating complexity and burn that the existing business can't support.</p><h2>Return on Invested Capital as the Allocation Guide</h2><p>In mature companies, capital allocation decisions are often guided by Return on Invested Capital (ROIC) — which investments generate the highest returns per dollar invested. For growth-stage companies, a simplified version of this discipline is equally powerful: for each major investment decision, estimate the expected revenue impact (with probability weighting for risk), the time to that impact, and the cost. The investments with the highest risk-adjusted, time-adjusted revenue per dollar invested should receive priority. This is not a perfect analytical exercise — the inputs involve significant uncertainty — but the discipline of going through the exercise forces explicit comparison of alternatives and prevents the most common allocation error: funding the loudest advocacy rather than the highest-return opportunity.</p>",
